## Read the minimum from the completed square
A square is never negative, so $2\left(x - \frac{7}{4}\right)^2$ is smallest (zero) when $x - \frac{7}{4} = 0$.
x - \frac{7}{4} &= 0
x &= \frac{7}{4}
## Find the minimum value
f\left(\frac{7}{4}\right) &= 2(0)^2 - \frac{129}{8}
&= -\frac{129}{8}
ANSWER: \left(\frac{7}{4},\; -\frac{129}{8}\right)

## Set the completed-square form equal to zero
2\left(x - \frac{7}{4}\right)^2 - \frac{129}{8} &= 0
2\left(x - \frac{7}{4}\right)^2 &= \frac{129}{8}
\left(x - \frac{7}{4}\right)^2 &= \frac{129}{16}
## Take the square root of both sides
x - \frac{7}{4} &= \pm\sqrt{\frac{129}{16}}
x &= \frac{7}{4} \pm \sqrt{\frac{129}{16}}
ANSWER: x = \frac{7}{4} \pm \sqrt{\frac{129}{16}}

## Find the modulus of z
|z| &= \sqrt{\left(-\sqrt{3}\right)^2 + 1^2}
&= \sqrt{4}
&= 2
## Find the argument of z
$z = -\sqrt{3} + i$ is in the second quadrant (negative real part, positive imaginary part). Find the reference angle $\alpha$ first.
\tan\alpha &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
\alpha &= \frac{\pi}{6}
\theta &= \pi - \frac{\pi}{6}
&= \frac{5\pi}{6}
## Write z in polar form
z &= 2\left(\cos\frac{5\pi}{6} + i\sin\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)
## Apply De Moivre's Theorem
$[r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)]^n = r^n(\cos n\theta + i\sin n\theta)$.
z^4 &= 2^4\left(\cos\frac{4\cdot 5\pi}{6} + i\sin\frac{4\cdot 5\pi}{6}\right)
&= 16\left(\cos\frac{10\pi}{3} + i\sin\frac{10\pi}{3}\right)
## Evaluate the cosine and sine
$\frac{10\pi}{3} - 2\pi = \frac{4\pi}{3}$, which is in the third quadrant, so both values are negative.
\cos\frac{10\pi}{3} &= -\frac{1}{2}
\sin\frac{10\pi}{3} &= -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
## Multiply out and simplify
z^4 &= 16\left(-\frac{1}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i\right)
&= -8 - 8\sqrt{3}\,i
ANSWER: z^4 = -8 - 8\sqrt{3}\,i

## Write f(g(x)) as a single function
f(g(x)) &= \ln\left(3(x+5)^2 + 2\right)
## Differentiate using the chain rule
$\frac{d}{dx}\ln u = \frac{u'}{u}$, where $u = 3(x+5)^2 + 2$.
u &= 3(x+5)^2 + 2
u' &= 6(x+5)
\frac{d}{dx}f(g(x)) &= \frac{6(x+5)}{3(x+5)^2 + 2}
## Substitute x = 1/4
x + 5 &= \frac{1}{4} + 5
&= \frac{21}{4}
\frac{d}{dx}f(g(x)) &= \frac{6\cdot\frac{21}{4}}{3\left(\frac{21}{4}\right)^2 + 2}
&= \frac{\frac{63}{2}}{\frac{1323}{16} + 2}
&= \frac{\frac{63}{2}}{\frac{1355}{16}}
&= \frac{504}{1355}
&\approx 0.372
ANSWER: 0.372

## Find the first perimeter
P_1 &= 4 \times 2
&= 8
## Find the second perimeter
A new square joins midpoints, so its side is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with legs $1$ and $1$.
\text{side} &= \sqrt{1^2 + 1^2}
&= \sqrt{2}
P_2 &= 4\sqrt{2}
## Find the common ratio
The perimeters $8,\ 4\sqrt{2},\ 4,\ \dots$ form a geometric series.
r &= \frac{4\sqrt{2}}{8}
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
## Use the sum to infinity
S_\infty &= \frac{a}{1-r}
&= \frac{8}{1 - \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}}
## Multiply top and bottom by $1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$
S_\infty &= \frac{8\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)}{\left(1 - \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)}
&= \frac{8\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)}{1 - \frac{1}{2}}
&= \frac{8\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)}{\frac{1}{2}}
&= 16\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)
## Expand and simplify
&= 16 + \frac{16}{\sqrt{2}}
&= 16 + 8\sqrt{2}
ANSWER: 16 + 8\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}

## Set p(t) equal to q(t)
1.1 e^{0.1t} \times 10^6 &= 3.9 e^{-0.05t} \times 10^6
1.1 e^{0.1t} &= 3.9 e^{-0.05t}
## Collect the powers of e on one side
\frac{e^{0.1t}}{e^{-0.05t}} &= \frac{3.9}{1.1}
Divide powers of the same base by subtracting the indices.
e^{0.1t - (-0.05t)} &= \frac{3.9}{1.1}
e^{0.15t} &= \frac{3.9}{1.1}
e^{0.15t} &= \frac{39}{11}
## Take natural logs and solve for t
0.15t &= \ln\frac{39}{11}
t &= \frac{\ln\frac{39}{11}}{0.15}
t &\approx 8.44 \text{ years}
$t = 8.44$ lies between $t = 8$ (start of 2018) and $t = 9$ (start of 2019), so it is during 2018.
ANSWER: 2018

## Write the loan as the sum of the repayments in today's money
A repayment $A$ made at the end of period $k$ is worth $\frac{A}{(1+i)^k}$ today. Today's value of all $t$ repayments is the loan $P$.
P &= \frac{A}{1+i} + \frac{A}{(1+i)^2} + \cdots + \frac{A}{(1+i)^t}
## Use the sum of a geometric series
This is a geometric series with first term $a = \frac{A}{1+i}$, common ratio $r = \frac{1}{1+i}$ and $t$ terms, so $S_t = \frac{a(1 - r^t)}{1 - r}$.
P &= \frac{\frac{A}{1+i}\left(1 - \left(\frac{1}{1+i}\right)^t\right)}{1 - \frac{1}{1+i}}
&= \frac{\frac{A}{1+i}\left(1 - \frac{1}{(1+i)^t}\right)}{1 - \frac{1}{1+i}}
## Simplify the denominator
1 - \frac{1}{1+i} &= \frac{1+i-1}{1+i}
&= \frac{i}{1+i}
## Cancel the factor 1/(1+i) and tidy up
P &= \frac{\frac{A}{1+i}\left(1 - \frac{1}{(1+i)^t}\right)}{\frac{i}{1+i}}
&= \frac{A\left(1 - \frac{1}{(1+i)^t}\right)}{i}
&= \frac{A\left(\frac{(1+i)^t - 1}{(1+i)^t}\right)}{i}
&= \frac{A\left((1+i)^t - 1\right)}{i(1+i)^t}
## Make A the subject
Multiply both sides by $i(1+i)^t$, then divide both sides by $(1+i)^t - 1$.
P\, i(1+i)^t &= A\left((1+i)^t - 1\right)
A &= \frac{P\, i(1+i)^t}{(1+i)^t - 1}
ANSWER: A = \frac{P\, i(1+i)^t}{(1+i)^t - 1}

## Rearrange the formula to get (1+i)^t on its own
Use $A = \frac{P\, i(1+i)^t}{(1+i)^t - 1}$.
A\left((1+i)^t - 1\right) &= P\, i (1+i)^t
A(1+i)^t - A &= P\, i (1+i)^t
A &= (1+i)^t\left(A - P\, i\right)
(1+i)^t &= \frac{A}{A - P\, i}
## Substitute A = 125, P = 5000 and i = 0.0165
(1.0165)^t &= \frac{125}{125 - 5000(0.0165)}
&= \frac{125}{42.5}
## Take logs and solve for t
\ln\left((1.0165)^t\right) &= \ln\left(\frac{125}{42.5}\right)
t \ln(1.0165) &= \ln\left(\frac{125}{42.5}\right)
t &= \frac{\ln\left(\frac{125}{42.5}\right)}{\ln(1.0165)}
t &\approx 65.92
t &= 66 \text{ months (to the nearest month)}
ANSWER: 66 \text{ months}
